---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const talkUrl = 'https://graphql.org/conf/2024/schedule/5245297ed1f7b82885c742d77f209bda/';
---
<Layout
  title="About GraphQL Stars"
  description="GraphQL Stars celebrates 52 people each year whose work across the GraphQL GitHub repositories moves the project forward."
  dark
>
  <section class="wrap page-intro stars-about-intro">
    <a class="back" href="/stars/">← Meet the GraphQL Stars</a>
    <p class="eyebrow">ABOUT GRAPHQL STARS</p>
    <h1>A universe shaped<br /><em>by contributors.</em></h1>
    <p class="standfirst">GraphQL Stars celebrates 52 people each year whose work across the GraphQL GitHub repositories moves the project forward.</p>
  </section>

  
  <div class="article-body stars-about-copy">
    <h2>GraphQL is a shared effort</h2>

  <p>
    GraphQL does not move forward by itself. Every improvement to the
    specification, its implementations, documentation, tests, tooling, and
    websites begins with someone choosing to contribute their time and
    knowledge.
  </p>

  <p>
    GraphQL Stars celebrates 52 of the people whose work across the GraphQL
    GitHub repositories helped the project move forward. It brings those
    contributors out from behind commits, issues, and pull requests, so the
    wider community can discover the people behind the work.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the Stars began</h2>

  <p>
    Benjie introduced GraphQL Stars at GraphQLConf 2024 during his
    keynote, <a href="https://graphql.org/conf/2024/schedule/5245297ed1f7b82885c742d77f209bda/?name=Keynote%3A%20You%27re%20Our%20Universe%3A%20GraphQL%20Community%20Update%202024"><cite>You’re Our Universe: GraphQL Community
    Update 2024</cite></a>. Looking back over a year of work across the
    project, he celebrated some of the people improving GraphQL and invited
    others to help shape what comes next.
  </p>

  <p>
    That celebration became GraphQL Stars: a new class of contributors,
    recognised each year.
  </p>
  </div>
<section class="wrap stars-video" aria-labelledby="talk-heading">
    <div class="stars-video-frame">
      <iframe
        src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/bCBZTNib0mA?si=yxipoNUAP8qZsM3v&amp;start=203"
        title="You're Our Universe: GraphQL Community Update 2024"
        loading="lazy"
        allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
        referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        allowfullscreen
      ></iframe>
    </div>
    <div class="stars-video-caption">
      <div>
        <h2 id="talk-heading">You're Our Universe: GraphQL Community Update 2024</h2>
        <p>Benjie Gillam · Community Gardener · GraphQLConf 2024</p>
      </div>
      <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bCBZTNib0mA">Watch on YouTube ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  
  <div class="article-body stars-about-copy">
  <h2>52 Stars, every year</h2>

  <p>
    Each class covers contributions made from September through August. The
    class of 2026, for example, reflects work carried out between September
    2025 and August 2026.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are 52 Stars in each class: one for every card in a deck. Explore
    the class, choose a card, and meet someone helping to build GraphQL.
  </p>

  <a class="button" href="/stars/">
    Explore the class of 2026 <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
  </a></div>
  
</Layout>
